AWS Terraform Infrastructure — Step-by-Step
1. Created VPC

Created the main VPC for the AWS infrastructure.

2. Created Public Subnets

Created two public subnets in different Availability Zones.

Used these subnets for the ALB and NAT Gateway.

3. Created Private Subnet

Created a private subnet for EC2 instances.

EC2 instances do not have direct internet access.

4. Created Internet Gateway

Attached an Internet Gateway to the VPC.

Provides internet connectivity to public subnets.

5. Created Public Route Table

Added a default route through the Internet Gateway.

Associated the route table with public subnets.

6. Created Elastic IP

Created an Elastic IP for the NAT Gateway.

7. Created NAT Gateway

Created the NAT Gateway inside the public subnet.

Allows private EC2 instances to access the internet for outbound traffic.

8. Created Private Route Table

Added a default route through the NAT Gateway.

Associated it with the private subnet.

9. Created Security Groups

Created an ALB security group allowing HTTP/HTTPS traffic.

Created an EC2 security group allowing application traffic from the ALB.

10. Created IAM Role

Created an IAM role for EC2 instances.

11. Created IAM Policies

Created two policies for required AWS permissions.

Attached the policies to the EC2 IAM role.

12. Created IAM Instance Profile

Created an instance profile to attach the IAM role to EC2 instances.

13. Created Launch Template

Configured AMI, instance type, security group, IAM instance profile, and user data.

14. Added User Data

Added a startup script to automatically install and configure the application when EC2 starts.

15. Created Auto Scaling Group

Created an ASG with minimum, maximum, and desired instance capacity.

Configured EC2 instances to launch in the private subnet.

16. Added Instance Refresh

Configured rolling instance refresh.

Allows EC2 instances to be replaced gradually when the Launch Template changes.

17. Created Target Group

Created an HTTP target group on port 80.

Configured health checks for EC2 instances.

18. Attached ASG to Target Group

Connected the Auto Scaling Group to the ALB target group.

New instances launched by the ASG are automatically registered.

19. Created Application Load Balancer

Created a public ALB in two Availability Zones.

ALB receives traffic from users and forwards it to private EC2 instances.

20. Created HTTP Listener

Created an HTTP listener on port 80.

Configured it to redirect HTTP traffic to HTTPS.

21. Created ACM Certificate for ALB

Created an ACM certificate in ap-southeast-1.

Added the main domain and www subdomain.

Used DNS validation.

22. Enabled HTTPS on ALB

Opened port 443 in the ALB security group.

Created an HTTPS listener on port 443.

Attached the ALB ACM certificate to the listener.

23. Created Private S3 Bucket

Created an S3 bucket for static/application files.

Kept the bucket private.

24. Enabled S3 Block Public Access

Blocked public access to the S3 bucket.

25. Created CloudFront Origin Access Control

Created OAC so CloudFront can securely access the private S3 bucket.

26. Created S3 Bucket Policy

Allowed CloudFront to read objects from S3.

Restricted access using the CloudFront distribution ARN.

27. Created ACM Certificate for CloudFront

Created a separate ACM certificate in us-east-1.

CloudFront requires its ACM certificate to be in us-east-1.

28. Validated CloudFront ACM Certificate

Used DNS validation.

Added the ACM CNAME records manually because Route 53 was not used.

Waited until the certificate became ISSUED.

29. Created CloudFront Distribution

Created CloudFront in front of the private S3 bucket.

Configured HTTPS and the CloudFront ACM certificate.

30. Configured CloudFront Price Class

Configured PriceClass_200 to provide broad coverage including Asia while avoiding the highest-cost PriceClass_All.

31. Configured CloudFront Domain

Added the main domain and www domain as CloudFront aliases.

32. Created CloudFront WAF

Created a WAF Web ACL with CLOUDFRONT scope.

Created it in us-east-1.

Added AWS managed security rules.

33. Attached WAF to CloudFront

Attached the CloudFront WAF to the CloudFront distribution.

34. Created ALB WAF

Created a separate WAF Web ACL with REGIONAL scope.

Created it in ap-southeast-1.

35.Attached WAF to ALB

Used aws_wafv2_web_acl_association.

Attached the regional WAF to the Application Load Balancer.

36. Created ALB Listener Rules

Created listener rules to route requests to the appropriate target groups.

Listener rules can route traffic based on conditions such as path or host.

37. Configured DNS

Did not use Route 53.

Configured the domain using an external DNS provider.

Pointed the domain to CloudFront/ALB as required.

38. Configured Terraform State and Drift Checking

Used Terraform state to track infrastructure managed by Terraform.

Used terraform plan to check for changes.

Used terraform plan -refresh-only to detect changes to Terraform-managed resources.

39. Configured AWS Config

AWS Config can be used to continuously record AWS resource configuration changes.

Useful for detecting changes made outside Terraform, including changes made through the AWS Console.

40. Configured CloudTrail

CloudTrail records AWS API activity.

Used to identify who made a change, when it happened, and which AWS API operation was performed.






Final Architecture

Internet → CloudFront → Private S3

Internet → ALB → Target Group → Private EC2

CloudFront WAF protects CloudFront.

ALB WAF protects the ALB.

ACM provides HTTPS certificates.

NAT Gateway provides outbound internet access to private EC2.

Auto Scaling Group manages EC2 instances.

AWS Config tracks infrastructure configuration changes.

CloudTrail provides an audit trail of AWS changes.
